{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 16, :comments ["<b>Beside me.</b> Joseph answered that the power of wisdom and understanding is not in me but outside of me — that is the meaning of בלעדי, that is to say, 'apart from me.' <b>Elohim will answer the peace of Pharaoh.</b> Everything comes from the power of Elohim, for He is the master of all powers, and He will help you from the distress of the dream and its interpretation. And he mentioned Elohim, because the answer in dreams comes from Him — and it is the final letter heh in the Name. So too Daniel said (Daniel 2:36), ״ופשרה נאמר קדם מלכא״ — the numerical value is 247, being the letters vav-heh, for how could one know the interpretation of a matter except through the two final letters, and understand this. For this reason He said, ״הוא גלי עמיקתא״ — for surely the heh and vav in the Name reveal the depth, drawing down the influx of the Beginning. And just as Joseph mentioned to Pharaoh 'beside me,' so too we find in Daniel, who said to Nebuchadnezzar (Daniel 2:30), ״ואנה לא בחכמה די איתי בי מכל חייא״ and so forth." "<b>And know</b> that I contemplate the matter of Pharaoh's dream, that it goes in seven upon seven. It is known that Pharaoh and his people are wicked, affirming the presuppositions of eternity, denying knowledge and providence and creation ex nihilo, and their view concerning all deeds done in the lower realm was that all are delivered to chance and under the governance of the planets that guide the world. And since he was drawn after nature and matter and the element of earth — as hinted in his name — they showed him from heaven these two dreams through the two heavy elements, which are water and earth: seven cows ascending from the element of water, and seven ears of grain sprouting from the element of earth. And the hint in them concerns the matter of plenty and famine, which is a proof of providence in the lower realm, as is mentioned concerning them at the head of the year. And from this they established in prayer, and concerning the lands it is said: which to famine and which to plenty. And this matter was a proof to Pharaoh from Him, blessed be He, concerning providence, to inform him that He, blessed be He, watches over the world of the elements and gives therein plenty and famine, and that He is the sovereign who rules over the seven planets, and in His hand to strengthen or weaken them according to recompense and punishment. And He needed to inform him of this because he made himself a god and said that the Nile was his river, as it is written (Ezekiel 29:3), ״לי יארי ואני עשיתני״. And the fact that the dream came from the Nile foreshadows the end from the beginning, to hint at his downfall at the start of the ten plagues, when the Nile was smitten with blood, and at his end, that he was destined to be smitten and destroyed in water if he did not turn back from this way of belief. And when he did not turn from his wickedness and stood in his rebellion, saying (Exodus 5:2), ״מי ה׳ אשר אשמע בקולו״, he was smitten with water, measure for measure. And because Joseph knew this evil belief of his, he hastened at the beginning of his words to mention to him the name Elohim, and said, 'beside Elohim' — that is to say, the wisdom of interpretation is not from my power but from the power of Elohim: He is the one who creates and watches and is able, and He will answer the peace of Pharaoh, that is to say, He will give help and assistance. For a man's answer of peace comes from Him, blessed be He, not from the side of the stars and constellations, and it is fitting for a man to direct his discourse to Him, not to them, as it is written (Job 5:8), ״ואל אלהים אשים דברתי״. And from this the verse says (Hosea 2:23–24), ״אענה נאם ה׳ אענה את השמים והם יענו את הארץ, והארץ תענה את הדגן ואת התירוש ואת היצהר״. The verse tells you that just as the earth has no answer at all without the answer of the heavens, so the heavens have no answer at all without the answer of HaShem, may He be exalted — and from this he mentioned here, 'Elohim will answer.' And with this intention Joseph mentions the name Elohim continually in all his words: he said, ״את אשר האלהים עושה הגיד לפרעה״. And he said further, ״אשר האלהים עושה הראה את פרעה״. And afterward he said, ״כי נכון הדבר מעם האלהים וממהר האלהים לעשותו״. He attributed the deeds to Elohim, to clarify that the deeds of man are not delivered to the supernal forces, as he supposes, but to the name Elohim. And therefore he opened at the beginning of his words with 'beside Elohim,' and concluded at the end of his discourse with 'and the Elohim makes haste to do it,' for he was speaking with Pharaoh, who denied the creation of the world in the act of the Beginning — and therefore he grasped the way of the act of the Beginning, which opens with בראשית ברא אלהים and concludes ״אשר ברא אלהים לעשות״." "<b>And you will find</b> in this parashah the counting of seven cows and seven ears of grain three times: one, the count of the Torah; the second, the count of Pharaoh to Joseph; and the third, the count of Joseph to Pharaoh. And three times fourteen are forty-two, and the counting will not remain void, for all of it was fulfilled in Egypt — from what the prophet said (Ezekiel 29:11), ״מצרים תהיה שממה ולא תשב ארבעים שנה״. For two years [the famine] began in Egypt, as it is said (Genesis 45:6), ״כי זה שנתים הרעב״, and afterward they completed to forty-two years, according to the calculation of the three counts in the Torah."], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 41, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:19:38.324308135Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}